# Highly Stable PhosphonateâBased MOFs with Engineered Bandgaps for Efficient Photocatalytic Hydrogen Production

## abstract
Photoactive metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) represent one of the most promising materials for photocatalytic hydrogen production, but phosphonate-based MOFs have remained largely underdeveloped compared to other conventional MOFs. Herein, a photocatalyst of 1D titanium phosphonate MOF is designed through an easy and scalable stirring hydrothermal method. Homogeneous incorporation of organophosphonic linkers can narrow the bandgap, which is due to the strong electron-donating ability of the uf8ffOH functional group that can efficiently shift the top of the valence band, moving the light absorption to the visible portion of the spectrum. In addition, the unique 1D nanowire topology enhances the photoinduced charge carrier transport and separation. Accordingly, the titanium phosphonate nanowires deliver remarkably enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity under irradiation of both visible light and a full-spectrum simulator. Such concepts of engineering both nanostructures and electronic states herald a new paradigm for designing MOF-based photocatalysts.

## Highly Stable Phosphonate-Based MOFs with Engineered Bandgaps for Efficient Photocatalytic Hydrogen Production
Photoactive metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) represent one of the most promising materials for photocatalytic hydrogen production, but phosphon­ ate-based MOFs have remained largely underdeveloped compared to other conventional MOFs. Herein, a photocatalyst of 1D titanium phosphonate MOF is designed through an easy and scalable stirring hydrothermal method. Homogeneous incorporation of organophosphonic linkers can narrow the bandgap, which is due to the strong electron-donating ability of the OH functional group that can efficiently shift the top of the valence band, moving the light absorption to the visible portion of the spectrum. In addition, the unique 1D nanowire topology enhances the photoinduced charge carrier transport and separation. Accordingly, the titanium phosphonate nanowires deliver remarkably enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity under irradiation of both visible light and a full-spectrum simulator. Such concepts of engineering both nanostructures and electronic states herald a new paradigm for designing MOF-based photocatalysts.
Sunlight-driven water splitting to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is an active field of energy research due to its promising potential to obtain renewable energy sources in a sustainable way.[1] One of the key requirements for achieving water photolysis with high “solar-tohydrogen” efficiencies is developing efficient photocatalysts.[2,3]
Unfortunately, in spite of intense research efforts in this area, the current photocatalysts for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation still confront several challenging issues:[1–4] 1) the catalysts suffer from low efficiency due to insufficient spatial separation efficiency of photoinduced charge carriers; 2) chemical doping is an efficient strategy to modify the catalysts’ electronic structures, resulting in reduced charge transfer barriers and extended absorbance range, though high energy consumption and tedious fabrication processes are needed; 3) a variety of conventional photocatalysts show poor stability (e.g., inorganic sulfide- and nitride-based photocatalysts are susceptible to oxidation); 4) many photocatalysts are made from expensive and rare components. Although all of these systems have beneficial features, the design of alternative photocatalysts with reduced complexity and high efficiency remains challenging.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) feature unique structures that consist of metal clusters or ions connected by organic linkers (e.g., organophosphonic acids, carboxylic acids), showing a high level of structural and functional tunability.[5,6] The metallic blocks mainly correspond to di/trivalent cations of 3d transition metals (e.g., Fe, Zn, Ni) and 3p metals,[5–9] among which Ti is an attractive candidate because of its relatively low cost, redox activity, and photocatalytic properties.[10–16] Incorporating Ti-oxo clusters delivers the semiconductor behavior of the MOFs with decent solar energy conversion efficiency, due to the facilitated electron transfer from the photoexcited organic linkers to the Ti-oxo clusters (i.e., linker-to-cluster charge transfer, termed as LCCT).[13–18] Particularly, bandgaps in the MOFs can be adjusted via modifying the organic linkers (e.g., introducing new functional groups), which can open up new possibilities to control the electronic structures/states of photo­ catalysts at the molecular level.[13–20] For instance, replacing a terephthalate linker in MIL-125 (a MOF constructed by terephthalate linker and Ti-oxo clusters) with the 2-aminoterephthalate linker could lead to a significantly decreased bandgap of MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .[13] Furthermore, judicious control over the facets of M $1\mathrm{IL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals could further improve photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activity as a result of increased percentage of exposed metal clusters.[12] This reveals that realizing the simultaneous adjustment of organic ligands and nanostructures can push the photocatalytic activity to a different level. However, most of the MOFs studied for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution are focused on carboxylate-based MOFs.[16–21] The poor coordination capability of carboxylate linkers poses major challenges in their operation stability (e.g., water or moisture stability). Phosphonate-based MOFs stand out among other MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis due to their high framework stability, which is related to the strong binding ability of phosphonates with metal clusters.[22] However, unlike the carboxylate-based MOFs, phosphonate-based MOFs for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution (e.g., titanium phosphonate MOFs), and especially the corresponding mechanism, have thus far been understudied.[23] Studies in open literatures are inclined to relate the improved photocatalytic performance to the increased light harvest,[14,23–26] while the electronic properties have not been thoroughly investigated yet. Understanding the structure–activity relationship for the phosphonate-based MOFs with engineered molecular structures in photocatalytic hydrogen production is envisioned to be significantly beneficial for the future design of photocatalytic systems.
Herein, titanium-phosphonate MOF based nanowires (hereafter referred to as TiPNW) are designed through a facile sol-gel chemistry and investigated in photocatalytic water splitting to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The synthesized TiPNW behaves as a semiconductor upon exposure to light irradiation, making a unique platform for active and stable water photolysis to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ while the electronic effect of the substituent groups in the organophosphonic linkers has a critical influence on the photocatalytic activity.
Well-defined nanostructures can ensure the accessibility of the active sites, minimize the photocarrier transport distance, and improve the mass transfer during the three-phase photo­ catalytic hydrogen evolution reaction, thus realizing a high photo­catalytic activity.[12,27,28] Nonetheless, the less controllable reaction rate between organophosphonic acids and metallic precursors (especially those with high valences, e.g., $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ ),[24,29] compared to the synthesis of carboxylate-based MOFs, makes the control over the nanostructures of metal phosphonates challenging to achieve. Here, a stirring hydrothermal technique was employed to prepare 1D titanium-phosphonatebased MOFs on the basis of sol-gel chemistry, and etidronic acid (EA) (Figure S1, Supporting Information), featuring easy post-modification and low cost, was used as the organophosphonic linker (Figure 1a). In contrast to classical hydrothermal method at a static state, reactants can be mixed well and a homogeneous reaction condition (e.g., temperature, reactant concentration) can be maintained in the stirring reaction system, providing a uniform hydrothermal condition for the formation of well-defined nanostructures.[30,31] Significantly, the mechanical force induced by the stirring can improve the mass transfer and direct the oriented assembly of titanium phosphonate oligomers along the axial direct, thus ensuring the formation of titanium phosphonate nanowires. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image in Figure  1b exhibits the high-yield nanowires with a large aspect ratio and an average diameter of ${\approx}80~\mathrm{nm}$ . The smooth surface of the nanowires observed by SEM is consistent with the observation from transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image (Figure  1c). The synthesized TiPNW is amorphous, as confirmed by the absence of diffraction rings in the selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure 1c) and the absence of crystalline lines in the high-resolution TEM image (Figure  1d). The uniform distribution of Ti, C, and P elements throughout the nanowires (energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images, Figure 1e) indicates the homogenous incorporation of Ti-oxo clusters and phosphonoic functional groups in the framework of TiPNW.
Noticeably, both the stirring condition and EA play indispensable roles in forming the 1D nanowire structure. The static condition led to the formation of titanium-phosphonate MOF nanoparticles (TiPNP) (Figure S2, Supporting Information), suggesting that the shear stress imposed by mechanical stirring could drive the self-assembly of titanium phosphonate oligomers along the axial direction. The simultaneous hydrothermal aging at stirring condition resulted in the ripening of the titanium phosphonate oligomers to produce 1D structure. However, only anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles could be obtained without using organophosphonic ligands even using the same stirring condition (Figure  2a and Figure S2, Supporting Information), which indicates the crucial role of the organic linkers in connecting oligomers to generate extended 1D nanostructures.
X-ray diffraction (XRD), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (MAS-NMR), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), and X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) were conducted to investigate the framework characteristics of TiPNW. In Figure 2a, only one broad hump appears in the wide-angle XRD range, indicating the amorphous feature of the synthesized TiPNW.[32] The extensive condensation reaction between metallic precursors and organophosphonic acids would favor the formation of an extensive metal–organic framework, while the high reaction rate and highly insoluble titanium phosphonate oligomers make them grow as noncrystalline nanowires.[22,30] The extensive titanium phosphonate skeleton is based on the $\mathrm{Ti{-}O{-}P}$ bonding that can connect organophosphonic bridging groups and Ti-oxo clusters, as confirmed by a characteristic stretching vibration at $1049\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to the $\mathrm{Ti{-}O{-}P}$ vibration in the FTIR spectrum (Figure S3, Supporting Information).[33]
In the $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ MAS-NMR spectrum (Figure  2b), a broadened, symmetric resonance peak appears at $18.8\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , a characteristic of noncrystalline metal phosphonates.[23,34] The $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MASNMR spectrum of TiPNW presents three resonance signals at 17.7, 66.2, and $57.1\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 2c), which correspond to the C atoms in terminal ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ , the quaternary C connected to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=O}$ , and the quaternary C connected to terminal $-\mathrm{OH}$ , respectively.[35] In the $^1\mathrm{H}$ MAS-NMR spectrum (Figure  2d), the two sharp signals at 1.5 and $4.2\ \mathrm{ppm}$ correspond to the hydrogen in $_\mathrm{C-OH}$ and terminal ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ , respectively.[36] Due to 49Ti’s low natural abundance, small gyromagnetic ratios, and low intrinsic detection sensitivity, double frequency sweep (DFS) enhanced $^{49}\mathrm{Ti}$ MAS-NMR technique was conducted to produce a higher signal-to-noise instead of conventional MAS-NMR. The DFS-enhanced MAS-NMR spectrum of TiPNW shows the absence of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ signal and the inherent feature of six-coordinated Ti atoms (Figure S4, Supporting Information). This indicates that all the Ti atoms in the framework of TiPNW form the Ti-oxo clusters, in which a Ti atom is bonded to six O atoms.
Importantly, a strong peak in the $\mathrm{~P~}2\mathrm{p}$ core level is attributed to $\mathrm{P}^{5+}$ in the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Ti-O-P}}$ bonding in the phosphonate-based MOFs (Figure  2e),[23,33,34] while the symmetric feature reveals the absence of defective $_\mathrm{P-OH}$ , which is further evidenced by the $^1\mathrm{H}$ MAS-NMR, $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ MAS-NMR, and FTIR spectra (i.e., lack of $\mathrm{\DeltaP\mathrm{-}O H}$ stretching vibration around $930~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , Figure S3, Supporting Information). Accordingly, the complete condensation between $_{\mathrm{P-O}}$ groups in the organophosphonic linkers and Ti-oxo clusters delivers a less defective, robust titanium phosphonate framework. The Ti 2p core level demonstrates a spin– orbit doublet (Figure  2f), which can be assigned to Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at $458.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ at $464.2\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Remarkably, compared to pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figures S5 and S6, Supporting Information), the Ti 2p core level for TiPNW shows a negative shift, which can be attributed to the electron-donative effect of the hydroxyl group.[17,20,36,37] This is an evidence for the influence of organic substituents on the electronic structure of the metal centers. The aforementioned results suggest that no phase separation occurred during the synthesis, and the organophosphonic units can efficiently cross-link Ti-oxo clusters to generate an extensive metal–organic framework (Figure S7, Supporting Information).[13,14,17,23,32]
We further conducted Ti K-edge XAFS measurements to probe the local atomic and electronic structures around the Ti atoms in TiPNW. In the near-edge region of Ti K-edge (Figure S8, Supporting Information), the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shows three pre-edge peaks, which can be related to the hybridization of d and p orbitals of the Ti atom in the octahedral ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{6}$ with surrounding atoms.[38,39] TiPNW exhibits a similar preedge feature to the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ except for decreased peak intensity. This indicates that the Ti atoms in the Ti-oxo clusters of TiPNW’s framework possess similar local coordination environments to the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , while the incorporation of organophosphonic ligands can modify the molecular orbital hybridization process (i.e., electronic structure), as confirmed in Ti 2p core level (Figure 2f). Fourier transform (FT) extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra can provide deep insights into the local geometrical structures (Figure S8, Supporting Information). The EXAFS spectrum for TiPNW bears a high resemblance to that of the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , indicating that the Ti-oxo clusters in the framework of TiPNW are composed of $\mathrm{TiO}_{6}$ octahedra. The intensity of peaks beyond $4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for TiPNW is much weaker than that for the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as expected for the crystalline anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ phase. Indeed, these peaks arise from the multiple scattering interactions in the outer shells of the oxo-bridged Ti ions with 3D connectivity.[40] This reveals that TiPNW has a much smaller size for the Ti-oxo clusters, making it possible for them to be connected by the organophosphonic ligands to deliver a 3D stable framework.
As expected, the introduction of functional groups in the organophosphonic linkers changes the optical response properties of TiPNW compared to the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . As given in the UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) (Figure  3a), TiPNW shows a bathochromic spectral shift to cover the visible region. According to the Tauc curves (inset of Figure  3a), the optical bandgap of TiPNW is calculated to be $2.71\mathrm{eV},$ smaller than that of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(2.99{\ \mathrm{~eV}})$ . This characteristic absorbance reveals the positive role of organophosphonic groups in efficiently modifying the bandgap and enhancing light harvesting.
Indeed, the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group is one of the strong electrondonating substituents. The $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups in the organophosphonate bridges can donate electron density to the antibonding orbitals of organic linkers,[17,20,41] which is capable of giving rise to the top level of the valence band (i.e., valence band maximum, VBM), thereby bringing the adsorption to the visible light range and decreasing the bandgap. The VBM positions of TiPNW and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are determined through linearly extrapolating the leading edges of valence band XPS (VB-XPS) curves to the baselines (Figure 3b).[42] TiPNW and anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ possess different VB positions with the edge of maximum energy at ${\approx}2.50\$ and $2.76\ \mathrm{eV}$ below Fermi level $\left(E_{\mathrm{F}}\right)$ , respectively. Compared to the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , TiPNW shows about $0.26\mathrm{eV}$ upwards shift in the VBM. To further address the band structure of the synthesized photocatalysts, the flat-band potential $(E_{\mathrm{fb}})$ was calculated based on Mott–Schottky measurements (Figure S9, Supporting Information). The positive slope of the Mott–Schottky plots indicates the n-type semiconductor behavior of TiPNW. Importantly, $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{fb}}}$ values of TiPNW and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are similar, $_{\approx-0.1\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus normal hydrogen electrode (NHE). Normally, the bottom of conduction band (i.e., conduction band minimum, CBM) of n-type semiconductors is about $0.1\mathrm{~V~}$ more negative than the $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{fb}}}$ [43,44] the CBM of the synthesized photocatalysts is determined to be around $-0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ versus NHE. This indicates that the CB is relatively insensitive to the organophosphonic groups, emphasizing the absence of insignificant long-range chemical interactions between organophosphonic linkers and a localized noninteracting state.[17,19] This effect is similar to previous carboxylatebased MOFs with electron-donating functional groups.[15,17,19,37] The much less sensitivity of the CB energy level is due to the fact that the CB character mainly relates to the $\mathrm{Ti}~3\mathrm{d}$ electrons or the orbitals delocalized over the whole organophosphonic linker.[17,37,45,46] Additionally, TiPNW exhibits a smaller slope than that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in the Mott–Schottky spectra, revealing a higher electron density and a faster charge transfer. The charge density $(N_{\mathrm{D}})$ calculated from the Mott–Schottky slopes is $5.19\times10^{18}$ and $1.64\times10^{18}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for TiPNW and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively, further confirming the positive role of electron-donating hydroxyl group in adjusting the electronic states.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were further conducted to illustrate the electronic states of the titanium phosphonate with different functional groups (Figure  4 and Figure S10, Supporting Information). Since the titanium phosphonate MOFs are amorphous in nature, we used a slab model to simulate their electronic states, while this model has been widely used to simulate other crystalline MOFs.[19,37] According to the projected density of states (Figure S10, Supporting Information), the CB of titanium phosphonate is mainly composed of Ti 3d orbitals. Noticeably, the incorporation of organophosphonic ligand can result in a decreased bandgap and a significantly elevated VBM, consistent with the experimental observations (Figure  3). This can be attributed to the strong electrondonating feature of $-\mathrm{OH}$ (Figure 4), which brings a stronger contribution to the VB of titanium phosphonate framework compared to that of $-\mathrm{Br}$ .
According to the results of Mott–Schottky plots and VB-XPS spectra, the bandgap values are determined to be 2.70 and $2.96\ \mathrm{\eV}$ for TiPNW and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively. The results are consistent with the bandgap values calculated from the UV– vis DRS spectra. The electronic band structures are depicted in Figure  3c. Noticeably, the band-edge positions reveal that TiPNW is thermodynamically favorable for photocatalytically producing hydrogen, since its CBM is located above the reduction potential of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the VBM is more positive than the oxidation potential of $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
The photocatalytic activity of the synthesized materials (loaded with $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles as the cocatalyst) was evaluated using the hydrogen evolution amount under different light irradiations (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The time course of hydrogen production under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>400~\mathrm{nm})$ is shown in Figure  5a. A decent hydrogen production of $1260~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is realized on TiPNW, obviously superior to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(255\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . In addition, TiPNP shows a lower photocatalytic activity of $501~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ than TiPNW, which reflects the critical role of well-defined 1D nanostructure in improving the hydrogen production performance.[42] Remarkably, the visible-light-driven activity of TiPNW can rival the state-of-the-art rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles with abundant oxygen vacancies $(932\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ,[46] and some efficient carboxylate-based MOFs with strong electron-donating substituents, such as MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ( $(367~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})^{[16]}$ and MIL-101(Cr)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ 1 $\cdot583\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[47] Upon exposure to fullspectrum simulator (i.e., simulated solar light) (Figure S12, Supporting Information), TiPNW delivers a high hydrogen evolution rate of $2346\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , about 2.47 and 4.07 times better than TiPNP $(955~\mathrm{\upmumol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}})$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(576~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , respectively. This is in good agreement with the photocatalytic activity trend detected under visible light illumination. Additionally, the apparent quantum yield of TiPNW is determined to be $0.77\%$ at $420~\mathrm{nm}$ , while it matches well with the optical absorption spectrum (Figure  5b), clearly suggesting that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is primarily produced by the photocatalytic process.[48,49]
The stability was studied by five consecutive cycles with $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ for each run. After the operation of five cycles, no significant activity degradation can be observed on TiPNW under visible light irradiations (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Note that TiPNW is able to maintain its photocatalytic activity without obvious deactivation even after storing it in the reaction system for two months (Figure S14, Supporting Information). No significant changes of framework and morphology can be observed before and after cycling tests (Figure S15, Supporting Information), confirming the robustness of the titanium phosphonate metal–organic framework in withstanding the harsh photocatalytic conditions.
During the photocatalytic reaction, the recombination rate of photoexcited electron–hole pairs should be slower than the charge carrier separation rate, which can enable the electrons to reduce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .[50,48] The charge separation-recombination efficiency was first investigated by photoluminescence (PL) spectra (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The strong emission in the PL spectrum of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ originates from the recombination of photoinduced electron–hole pairs,[51,52] whereas a remarkable PL quenching of TiPNW suggests a highly increased charge separation efficiency. Importantly, in comparison to the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the PL spectrum of TiPNW shows an obvious redshift of the PL peak. This phenomenon can be related to the electron transfer process or/and charge delocalization between the organic functional groups and the Ti-oxo clusters (Figure  4), since the empty orbitals and the strong Lewis acidity of $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ can promote the electron withdrawal from the organophosphonic linkers, thus leading to a redshift of the photogenerated electron transfer.[53,54] This effect is similar to the previously reported $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{Uio}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ with $Z\mathrm{r}$ -oxo clusters.[18]
Indeed, the separation of electrons and holes can be facilitated by the high electron mobility. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement manifests a much decreased arc diameter on TiPNW (Figure S17, Supporting Information), which can be related to the increased interfacial electron mobility.[15,48] After normalizing hydrogen production rate by specific surface area (Figure S18, Supporting Information), TiPNW shows a normalized rate of $0.394\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{m}^{-2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ still outperforming TiPNP $(0.075\mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}\ m^{-2}\ g^{-1}})$ , though TiPNP has a larger surface area. This reveals that the photocatalytic activity is not simply related to the surface area but also intimately associated with the nanostructure. In addition, the surface photovoltage spectroscopy (SPS) response of TiPNW outperforms other control photocatalysts (Figure S19, Supporting Information). Although TiPNW and TiPNP possess the same framework compositions, the smaller charge transfer resistance and larger surface photovoltage of TiPNW reveal that 1D nanowires formed by intimate link between organophosphonic units and Ti-oxo clusters can provide a continuous pathway for the efficient transport of photoinduced electrons. The resultant high electron transfer rate is beneficial for the efficient electron–hole separation during the photocatalytic reaction.
To further illustrate the electron transfer, the transient photocurrent responses in different electrolytes were conducted to evaluate the interfacial charge kinetics. According to the photocurrent response plots (Figure 5c) recorded in the acetonitrile (ACN) electrolyte, TiPNW exhibits an instant and reversible response upon light switching, wherein the photocurrent relates to the transfer of photoinduced electrons to electrode.
After introducing water into the ACN electrolyte, the photocurrent density is reduced. The decrease of photocurrent responses is associated with the competitive interfacial charge transfer to the adsorbed water molecules. Noticeably, the photocurrent reduction of TiPNW $(17\%)$ is higher than that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(7\%)$ , revealing a favorable electron transport to the adsorbed water molecules for TiPNW. Furthermore, a lower overpotential for water reduction to produce hydrogen and a more pronounced cathodic current can be observed on TiPNW in the polarization curves (Figure S20, Supporting Information), implying an enhanced reaction kinetics (i.e., increased electronic conductivity and facilitated water reaction reaction). The organic–inorganic hybrid nature of the synthesized titaniumphosphonate-based MOFs in this work can take advantage of the increased light harvesting during the photocatalytic reaction. In the meantime, the structural superiority can improve the transfer rate of photoinduced electrons and the separation efficiency of photoinduced electron–hole pairs, as the homogeneous titanium phosphonate framework and 1D nanostructures decrease the charge diffusion distance and enhance the electron relocalization on the surface active sites so as to inhibit the charge recombination.[42,51]
When the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group in TiPNW was replaced by the $-\mathrm{Br}$ group through a post-modification method (see experimental details in the Supporting Information, the Br-functionalized TiPNW is denoted as TiPNW-Br, Figure  5d and Figure S21, Supporting Information), the photocatalytic hydrogen production activity of TiPNW-Br is depressed compared to the pristine TiPNW (Figure 5a and Figure S12, Supporting Information). The poorer efficiency of the TiPNW-Br derivative under the same conditions proves that the photocatalytic activity for hydrogen production over TiPNW is mainly related to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionality. This result is related to the fact that the $-\mathrm{Br}$ group with weaker electron-donating ability could make limited contribution to the electron filling of the antibonding orbitals,[17,20,37] which leads to a lower VBM level and a larger bandgap in comparison to TiPNW (Figure  3c). The Ti-oxo clusters within TiPNW are uniformly spaced out by the organophosphonic ligands, while the incorporation of functional substituents into the organophosphonic linkers can influence the electronic structure of the hybrid framework. Upon light irradiation, the photoinduced electrons transfer from their excited states to the CB of the Ti-oxo clusters via a LCCT mechanism, forming ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ states and holes at the organic ligands (Figure 5e).[14,16,21,55] Subsequently, the Pt cocatalyst can spatially separate the electrons from the organic linkers, while the corresponding holes react with the electron donor reagent (triethanolamine, TEOA), thus achieving the charge carrier separation and improving the hydrogen evolution on Pt.
In conclusion, a template-free method was developed to prepare a new 1D titanium phosphonate photocatalyst. This photocatalyst exhibits decent photocatalytic hydrogen production and remarkable stability, which are intimately related to the robust titanium phosphonate framework with decreased bandgap and structural superiority for efficient charge carrier separation and transportation. This study demonstrates the possibility of tuning the electronic structures of phosphonate-based MOFs through rationally functionalizing the organic ligands and opens up a new way to develop advanced photocatalysts with designed structural and electronic features.
